# Phone

Phase 1 resource: `server/resources/[gameplay]/phone/` — it makes the
inventory's `phone` item real: every character gets a phone number, can text
other characters, and can call players who are online. It follows the same
model as economy, inventory, and the marketplace: **server-authoritative**.
Clients can only *request* actions (text / call / answer / view); the Lua
server script validates every step. A modded client cannot text as someone
else, read another character's inbox, spoof a caller, or invent a number.

## How numbers work

- **Derived, not assigned:** a character's number is a hash (djb2) of their
  **license**, formatted `<area>-555-<4 digits>` — e.g. `1017-555-0123`.
  The area code is `1017` (Atlanta) when the hash is even, `312` (Chicago)
  when it is odd. The same license always produces the same number, across
  restarts and sessions, with no number table to store, sync, or steal —
  and no way for a player to pick or change their number.
- **Registry:** a number only becomes reachable once its owner has actually
  played — the server registers a character when their character loads and
  the first time they use the phone. Texting a number nobody has ever
  played on returns "No such number."
- **The item is the gate:** sending a text or placing a call requires
  actually carrying the `phone` item (`inventory/shared/items.lua` is its
  single source). Receiving texts and calls always works. Lose the phone,
  lose the sending — the item finally does something.

## Texts

- **Live delivery:** if the target is online, the text lands immediately
  (`Text from <name> (<number>): ...`) and is also stored in their inbox.
- **Offline delivery:** if the target has played before but is offline, the
  text is queued into their inbox. On their next login they are told
  "You have N text(s) waiting — /inbox."
- **Inbox:** keyed to the license, not the session, so texts survive the
  recipient logging out and back in. `/inbox` prints the most recent
  `InboxShow` of up to `InboxCap` stored texts (oldest are dropped past the
  cap). Maximum text length is `MaxTextLength`; longer texts are rejected,
  never silently truncated.

## Calls

- **Ringing:** `/call <number>` requires the target to be online and not
  already on the phone. The target sees who is calling and can `/answer`
  or `/decline`; an unanswered ring times out after `RingSeconds` and the
  caller and target are both told.
- **Talking:** an answered call connects exactly two players. `/c <message>`
  sends a line to the call partner — and nobody else — and echoes it back
  to the speaker.
- **Ending:** `/hangup` ends a call (or cancels your outgoing ring). If a
  player disconnects mid-call or mid-ring, the partner is told the other
  side disconnected. Calls are live state only; there is nothing to save.

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `/phone` | Show your number and a one-line help summary |
| `/contacts` | List online players with their numbers (never shows licenses) |
| `/text <number> <message>` | Text a character, e.g. `/text 1017-555-0123 running late, omw` — delivered live or queued to their inbox |
| `/inbox` | Read your stored texts (newest last) |
| `/call <number>` | Ring an online player |
| `/answer` | Answer an incoming call |
| `/decline` | Decline an incoming call |
| `/hangup` | End a call, or cancel your outgoing ring |
| `/c <message>` | Talk to your call partner (only while in a call) |

## Tuning (`shared/config.lua`)

- `RequirePhoneItem` (default `true`) — sending requires carrying the
  `phone` item. Receiving always works.
- `RingSeconds` (default `30`) — how long a call rings before timing out.
- `MaxTextLength` (default `160`) — longest sendable text, in characters.
- `InboxCap` (default `50`) — texts kept per character; oldest drop off.
- `InboxShow` (default `10`) — texts `/inbox` prints at once.

## Exports (for other resources)

- `exports.phone:GetNumber(source)` — the character's derived phone number
  (or `nil` if their character is not loaded).
- `exports.phone:SendText(source, toNumber, message)` — the same validated
  send path `/text` uses; returns a boolean. Other resources (job dispatch,
  marketplace sale alerts) can text a character without re-implementing any
  of the checks.

## Not built yet (expansion seams, in rough order)

- **Persistence:** v1 inboxes and the registry are in memory — a server
  restart wipes stored texts and forgets who has ever played (numbers
  themselves are derived, so they never change). Fine for testing, not for
  a public launch. The fix is API tables written through the core bridge
  (`phone_inboxes`, `phone_registry`), the same road inventory persistence
  rides.
- **Real voice:** calls are text lines (`/c`) in v1. The seam is a voice
  bridge (pma-voice / saltychat style) keyed to the same call state — the
  ring/answer/hangup flow here becomes the call setup there.
- **An NUI phone** on the HUD instead of chat output: contacts app, message
  threads, keypad. The server events are already shaped for it.
- **Blocking and unknown callers:** a per-character block list, and calls
  from numbers not in your contacts showing as the raw number only.
- **Group texts / dispatch channels** for jobs (police/EMS radio as a
  phone channel, reusing the inbox store).
